<Docs slug="pick-a-folder">
  <p>
    An empty window has one button: <strong>Choose Folder</strong>. Pick the
    folder holding the shoot you want to work on. LightPhotos scans it and
    fills the grid right away &mdash; and there is no import step, nothing to
    copy into a library, no catalog to wait for. The files stay exactly where
    they are.
  </p>

  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>The web build asks for permission.</strong> Your browser wants
      confirmation before any page may read a folder. Grant it for the folder
      you picked; that is the only access LightPhotos gets, and it covers one
      folder, not your whole drive.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Switch folders whenever you like.</strong> Click
      <strong>Open</strong> in the header, or press <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>O</kbd>.
      Work on one shoot, then point it at the next.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your settings live next to your photos.</strong> Ratings, labels
      and edits are written into a <code>.lightphotos</code> folder inside the
      folder you opened. Move the folder and the edits travel with it; delete
      that folder and you lose the edits, never the photos. There is no
      database somewhere else holding your work hostage.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Only some files show up.</strong> The grid lists the formats
      LightPhotos can open &mdash; see
      <a href="/docs/formats.html">supported formats</a> for the full set and
      for what else gets written to disk.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The scan happens in the background. Thumbnails decode as they scroll into
    view, so you can start rating the first frames while the rest of the folder
    is still being worked out &mdash;
    <a href="/docs/browse.html">how browsing works</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
